  "summary": "Flat tiles, giant type, motion that means it: software as Swiss signage.",
  "description": "Metro — Microsoft's design language for Zune, Windows Phone 7 and Windows 8 — rebuilt UI around typography and motion: flat color tiles of live content, huge segmentation-lettered headers, no ornament, and animations with physical authority (slides that decelerate, pivots with weight). Its Swiss/International Typographic lineage was explicit (signage, transport graphics), and at its best (Windows Phone 7-8, Zune HD) it was the most distinctive consumer UI of its decade. Windows 8's desktop transplant failed publicly; Microsoft moved to Fluent, but Metro's typographic discipline — type as interface, chrome as nothing — remains foundational to flat design's best practice.",
  "culturalContext": "Metro was Microsoft's Swiss signage moment: typography-first tiles and weighted motion made Windows Phone the decade's most distinctive UI, Windows 8's failure ended its reign, but Metro's discipline — type as interface, chrome as nothing — became flat design's best-practice foundation.",
